[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Developing Custom Java Samplers and Plugins in Apache JMeter"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Apache JMeter is extremely extensible. When standard samplers like HTTP or JDBC do not meet your protocol requirements, you can develop custom Java samplers by implementing the JavaSamplerClient interface."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites and Setup"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Java Development Kit (JDK 8 or higher)"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Apache Maven or Gradle for dependency management"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Apache JMeter binaries installed locally"},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 1: Create a Maven Project"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a new Maven project and add the core JMeter dependencies to your pom.xml file. Ensure you use the version matching your JMeter runtime."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"<dependency>
    <groupId>org.apache.jmeter</groupId>
    <artifactId>ApacheJMeter_core</artifactId>
    <version>5.5</version>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.apache.jmeter</groupId>
    <artifactId>ApacheJMeter_java</artifactId>
    <version>5.5</version>
</dependency>"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 2: Implement the JavaSamplerClient Interface"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a new class that extends AbstractJavaSamplerClient. You will need to override getDefaultParameters, setupTest, runTest, and teardownTest."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"package com.example.jmeter.samplers;

import org.apache.jmeter.config.Arguments;
import org.apache.jmeter.protocol.java.sampler.AbstractJavaSamplerClient;
import org.apache.jmeter.protocol.java.sampler.JavaSamplerContext;
import org.apache.jmeter.samplers.SampleResult;

public class CustomProtocolSampler extends AbstractJavaSamplerClient {

    private static final String FUNCTION_PARAM = "FUNCTION_NAME";

    @Override
    public Arguments getDefaultParameters() {
        Arguments defaultParameters = new Arguments();
        defaultParameters.addArgument(FUNCTION_PARAM, "default_action");
        return defaultParameters;
    }

    @Override
    public SampleResult runTest(JavaSamplerContext context) {
        String functionName = context.getParameter(FUNCTION_PARAM);
        SampleResult result = new SampleResult();

        result.sampleStart();
        try {
            // Perform your custom protocol logic here
            Thread.sleep(100);
            result.setResponseData("Success executing: " + functionName, "UTF-8");
            result.setSuccessful(true);
            result.setResponseMessage("OK");
        } catch (Exception e) {
            result.setSuccessful(false);
            result.setResponseMessage(e.getMessage());
        } finally {
            result.sampleEnd();
        }

        return result;
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 3: Build and Deploy"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Package your project into a JAR file using Maven (mvn clean package). Copy the generated JAR file into the 'lib/ext' directory of your Apache JMeter installation."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 4: Use in JMeter"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Launch JMeter and add a Thread Group."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Add a 'Java Request' sampler under the Thread Group."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Select your custom class 'com.example.jmeter.samplers.CustomProtocolSampler' from the dropdown list."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Configure parameters and run your test plan."}]
